Only separation is seeded because it was measured to be the only random stage: on the sample video, two unseeded separations differed while transcription and diarization each gave identical output when run twice on the same vocals (F5-TTS already takes an explicit per-attempt seed). The seed is set inside separate_vocals rather than once at startup so a resumed run (--from-stage separate) gets the same vocals as a full run, regardless of what ran earlier in the process; the test seeds random differently before each call to prove that. Seeding the global random is necessary because demucs.apply calls random.randint directly and offers no seed or RNG parameter. Setting shifts=0 would also make it deterministic, but changes separation quality, so the default stays shifts=1 and DEMUCS_SHIFTS exposes it.
